WEBVTT

NOTE
Same Call, Charted Twice: the spoken lines. Dramatization with practice data; the voices are AI-generated.

1
00:00:01.460 --> 00:00:03.960
Medic 1: BP's 128 over 76!

2
00:00:04.010 --> 00:00:05.280
Medic 2: Fentanyl is in!

3
00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:06.300
Medic 1: What time was that?

4
00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:07.310
Medic 2: I think it's…

5
00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:08.530
Medic 1: I don't have paper.

6
00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:09.770
Medic 2: Write it on the glove.

7
00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:11.020
Medic 2 (intercom): Zofran's in!

8
00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:13.370
Medic 1 (intercom): Sinus tach on the 12-lead!

9
00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:15.300
Medic 1: Was it 50, or 100?

10
00:00:15.350 --> 00:00:18.310
Medic 2: Was that 14:05? Or 14:07?

11
00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:20.120
ED nurse: How much fentanyl? And when?

12
00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:24.450
Medic 2: Fifty, I think. I think the time was… 14:05?

13
00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:29.800
Medic 2: I think it was… around 14:05?

14
00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:31.140
Medic 2: Close enough.

15
00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:34.780
Narrator: Now, the same call. With ARCTIN.

16
00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:39.150
On the ground or in the air, chart it once, at the patient.

17
00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:41.780
Every time is stamped as it happens.

18
00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:50.179
ARCTIN Hub builds the timeline, and the narrative, from what you charted.

19
00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:55.700
With your agency's approval, the receiving hospital can follow the case before you arrive.

20
00:00:55.750 --> 00:01:00.900
Medical directors and clinical leaders can follow the case, and so can the receiving hospital.

21
00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:05.267
The crew answers their messages with a tap, and it all lands in the chart.

22
00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:12.191
ARCTIN records and reminds. Your crew makes every care decision.

23
00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:14.950
So why would you chart any other way?

24
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.797
ARCTIN. Every Second. Every Life.
